WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.450
On a charter, the biggest stability problem isn’t fuel or ice.

2
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.300
It’s people.

3
00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:08.025
Picture it.

4
00:00:08.275 --> 00:00:14.750
Somebody hooks a big kingfish on the port side, and all six passengers rush to the port rail to watch.

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.875
On a small boat, that alone can swamp or capsize her.

6
00:00:19.125 --> 00:00:22.300
So what do you say before lines go in the water?

7
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.025
Stay spread out.

8
00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:28.175
One or two at the rail, the rest stay put.

9
00:00:28.425 --> 00:00:33.300
Keep heavy things, coolers, ice and gear, low and near the centerline.

10
00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.875
On small boats, respect the capacity plate.

11
00:00:37.125 --> 00:00:40.325
And remember that your loading changes all day.

12
00:00:40.575 --> 00:00:44.775
You burn off fuel, the ice melts, and the fish box fills up.

13
00:00:45.025 --> 00:00:48.275
The boat you bring home isn’t the boat you took out.

14
00:00:48.525 --> 00:00:54.425
Finally, water trapped on deck is free surface, right where you can least afford it: up high.
